// en-druid — English with four hundred years of Gaelic under it.
//
// ANCIENT sat on en-gb-x-rp, which is Received Pronunciation: a broadcast accent
// standardised in the 1920s. For a voice that is meant to predate it by four
// centuries that is not merely wrong, it is backwards — RP is the youngest
// English in the cast.
//
// The base is en-gb-scotland, which is the nearest thing espeak-ng has to a
// Gaelic substrate: rhotic, with the vowel system Scots English kept and RP
// lost. Only the features that survive in Highland and Hebridean English are
// taken, and each is one several speakers share rather than one speaker's habit.

name en-druid
language en-druid 8
language en 22

// `phonemes` TAKES A TABLE, NOT A VOICE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS SILENT.
//
// This said `phonemes en-gb-scotland`. That is a VOICE name — there is no
// phoneme table called that — and espeak-ng does not complain: it resolves
// nothing and every word in the probe comes back as `@@@_::`, schwas with the
// length marks still attached. Not an error, not silence, a voice with no words
// in it. Tested afterwards: en-sc, en-gb-sc, en-n, en-rp, en-wm and even en-wi
// all fail the same way HERE, while en-wi works in en-earth — the difference is
// the priority number on the first `language` line, which changes how the table
// is resolved. `phonemes en` is the shape that works with a numbered first line,
// and it is what en-zen and en-t800 already use.
//
// So the Gaelic is carried by the RULES below and by the timing above, not by a
// borrowed table. That is the honest way round anyway: the substrate features
// are what a listener hears, and a table would have brought a whole accent when
// only three features survive four hundred years.
phonemes en

// SLOW, AND EVENLY SO.
// Gaelic-substrate English is far less stress-timed than RP — the unstressed
// syllables keep their length instead of collapsing. That evenness is most of
// what reads as old: modern English hurries between its stresses, and this does
// not. Long across the board, and a narrow spread between the levels.
stressLength 210 210 215 215 240 240 258 272

// Emphasis barely moves. Wisdom does not lean on words.
stressAmp 14 14 15 15 16 17 18 19

// A GAP BETWEEN WORDS. Wider than any other voice here except H.A.L's. The
// spacing is what turns slow into DELIBERATE, and it is the difference between
// someone old and someone tired.
words 4

dictrules 8

// THE THREE FEATURES THAT SURVIVE.
//
// The tapped /r/ is the single most identifiable feature of Gaelic-influenced
// English and the last to go in speakers who have otherwise lost the substrate.
replace 00 r *

// Gaelic has no /z/ — it is heard and produced as /s/, and this is the second
// most durable feature of the substrate.
replace 00 z s

// The dental fricatives are not in the Gaelic inventory either; /th/ goes to a
// dental stop rather than to /s/ (which is what a Germanic substrate does — see
// en-t800). Kept voiced-for-voiced so no word changes: this is the whole test a
// replace has to pass.
replace 00 T t
replace 00 D d
